2:title: "Where a recording lands"
25:The recording goes in at the chosen project's playhead, in whatever is open there. If you're working inside a [nest](/editing/nests/), it lands inside the nest.
27:| What was recorded | Where it lands |
48:Everything that lands in an open project is one undo step, **Add Recording**. **Edit ▸ Undo Add Recording** removes the clips and their media entries and leaves the files on disk, as undoing **Add Media…** does.
50:Anything set up in [When it lands](/recording/when-it-lands/) runs after the landing as its own undo steps, so those come off first.
86:A recording that lands cleanly doesn't bring the panel back. Once it has landed, press <kbd>⌥⌘N</kbd> to open it. While a recording runs, the same keys stop it.
109:- A project already at its limit of 64 tracks can't take a recording that needs a new track. The picture and the microphone land together or not at all, so if the picture would fit and the microphone wouldn't, nothing lands. The files stay on disk and the note names them.
116:`record_screen` lands a recording the same way, with two differences. With no project open it always opens a new project. And it can add a recording to the media list without placing a clip, which the panel can't, though a new project's picture is on the timeline regardless. See [Record with record_screen](/mcp/record-screen/).
docs/site/src/content/docs/mcp/tools-recording.mdx:71:The call blocks for the whole recording, then until the files are closed and landed. If the files are slow to close it waits up to 75 s, and 60 s more for each further segment of a paused recording, or 105 s and 90 s with a camera beside the screen. `enhance` and `speed_up_typing` each wait up to 5 minutes more for the analysis they need. It answers `path`, `media`, `clip`, `duration`, `width`, `height`, `fps`, `has_audio`, `audio_tracks`, `mic_offset`, `pauses`, `segments` with one entry for each file, `stopped_by` and `landed`, plus the microphone's and the camera's own keys when they're used, and a `note` when there's something to say. Each polish step adds its own answer under its name, or a sentence under `<name>_error`, such as `auto_zoom_error`.
